# diagnostics.tkr — the CHECKER-STAGE compile-fail CHANNEL.
#
# The project it names is still here and still asserted (`m1::PRIV`, #594 crumb 8c / issue #613),
# and it now carries the `member_const_visibility_leak_rejected` regression too (`leakbase`/
# `leaksub`, #594 crumb 7) — that project became the PARSE-stage host and can no longer reach the
# checker at all, so its assertion had to move to a build that does. Around those two, FORTY-FOUR
# further rejected constructs live one per namespace under `src/` (thirty-seven at the all-
# diagnostics ruling, plus `c61_unsafe_alias_rejected` folded in by cargo/20-unsafe-alias-rejeitado,
# plus FIVE more folded in by cargo/20-c-types-impl — `c62`, `c63`, `c65`, `c66`, `c67`, the reject
# half of docs/design/c-types-and-marshalling-0.3.1.md, §11), each a fixture that used to be a
# `Given source inline` snippet in regressor.tkr paying for its OWN failing build. (A SIXTH,
# `c64_c_string_in_safe_signature_rejected`, was folded in here too but has since moved to the
# SIGNATURE walk's own build below — its HARD boundary was cutting this project's whole item walk
# before the item walk ever ran, silently dropping every diagnostic past the pre-loop passes,
# `is private to` included; see the SIGNATURE walk Feature's note.)
#
# WHY THEY SHARE ONE BUILD (owner ruling 2026-07-25 — "somente um build é necessário"): the runner
# already supports it. A compile-fail scenario that declares NO `Given source`/`Given source inline`
# is checked against the PROJECT's own failing build, and that build is cached across every scenario
# of this file (`tkr_compile_fail_artifact` → `tkr_ensure_built`). The compiler reports EVERY
# diagnostic it finds rather than stopping at the first, so one build emits every ITEM-WALK
# diagnostic and each scenario below pins its own — SO LONG AS no HARD pre-walk boundary (the
# SIGNATURE walk) sits among them; one did (`c64`, above) until this pass moved it out. Scenario/
# build counts in this header predate that move and are flagged, not silently re-derived, per this
# file's own "measured, not assumed" discipline — the next full remeasurement re-confirms them.
#
# ONE MORE NAMESPACE ADDED since that measurement (`c62_arena_size_on_unsafe_rejected`, #453's
# `arena_size_on_unsafe_rejected` fixture — numbered c62, not c61: c61 was already taken by a
# sibling cargo's `c61_unsafe_alias_rejected` merged into this branch while this one was in
# flight) — a FILE inside this project, per the channel rule (variation in SOURCE needs a
# separate file, not a separate build); it shares the SAME failing build as the other thirty-nine
# and does not move the "1 build" figure. The scenario count above is therefore stale by one until
# the next full remeasurement re-confirms it — flagged rather than silently bumped, per this
# file's own "measured, not derived" discipline.
#
# WHY TWO MORE FOLDED IN (issue #68). `c55_unsafe_field_in_safe_struct` and `c53_reject_flags_65`
# were two of the four `cases/*.tks` rows that used to buy their own build, because their
# diagnostics come from a checker PRE-WALK pass that stopped at the first error. Those two passes
# (`validate_type_decls` and `check_modules`) are SOFT boundaries — they produce nothing the item
# walk reads — so they now collect and the build carries on. They are namespaces of the shared build.
#
# WHY ONE `cases/*.tks` ROW SURVIVES (`prewalk_signature_stops.tks`) — AND WHY A THIRD CONSTRUCT
# JOINED IT. The surviving constructs are rejected by the checker's SIGNATURE walk, a HARD boundary:
# a declaration whose signature is rejected contributes no binding, so continuing would report every
# caller as "undefined" and blame the caller for the callee's defect (M.3). The signature walk
# therefore collects everything IT sees and cuts. `c64_c_string_in_safe_signature_rejected`
# (`reject_unsafe_signature_contagion`, cargo/20-c-types-impl) is the SAME HARD boundary as
# `discard_param_plain_fn`/`ref_depth_rejected` but was originally folded into THIS project's shared
# item-walk build instead — a placement mistake found while chasing why `is private to` (the
# `m1::PRIV` scenario, two Features up) had stopped appearing: `c64`'s HARD signature failure cut
# the pre-loop passes before the item walk that produces `is private to` ever ran, and every OTHER
# item-walk diagnostic in this project's forty-plus scenarios went missing the same way, silently.
# All three constructs now sit in ONE file and are reported by ONE build, and a standalone `Given
# source` build is cached per (file, declared env) — `regr_src_key`. Residual: one build, was four.
# See docs/design/all-diagnostics-pre-walk-0.3.1.md §3 for every pass's boundary.
#
# TWO NAMESPACES LEFT THIS CHANNEL on 2026-07-27, and the reason is a ruling, not a cleanup.
# `c40_redundant_cast_same_type` and `c58_d1_class3_noop_widen_still_rejected` both pinned the D1
# `redundant cast` diagnostic while it was a hard ERROR. The owner reversed that ruling — *"o cast,
# nestes casos, não deveria ser falha, deveria ser warning, o que não nos exime de não ter warnings
# no próprio compilador (lembra? <= 2%)"* — so both constructs now COMPILE, emitting a stderr
# warning. A construct that no longer fails cannot be asserted by a channel whose whole contract is
# `When compilation fails`, so the namespaces and their scenarios were deleted rather than left to
# assert a diagnostic this build no longer produces.
#
# THE COVERAGE DID NOT GO WITH THEM. Both classes moved to `src/checker/checker_test.tkt` as unit
# tests of the new behaviour — `d1_class1_same_type_cast_keeps_its_node` (class 1, and that the
# `TCast` node survives so the D4 ≤2% gate still counts it) and
# `d1_class3_noop_widen_warns_but_types_arith`/`_compare` (class 3, at BOTH diagnostic sites).
# The ceiling that now carries the policy is D4's ARITH-CAST-RATE ≤ 2%, which still FAILS a build.
#
# THE MEASUREMENT THAT DECIDED IT, kept because it is the reason the remaining row is NOT folded and
# because it refutes the obvious-looking move. Taken while all four still stopped at the first error:
#
#   * put all four in a project of their own and the build reports exactly ONE diagnostic; remove
#     that one and the build reports the next, alone — a pass that STOPS makes four cases in one
#     build one diagnostic, not four;
#   * fold ONE of them into THIS project and the build's diagnostic count falls from FIFTY to ONE.
#     Every scenario below would have gone red to save a single build.
#
# Two of the four stopped being a problem by being FIXED, not by being folded: their passes now
# collect. The surviving row is the one whose boundary is HARD by design, so folding it would still
# cost the fifty. It stays a build until the signature walk can collect too — the all-diagnostics
# work, not this channel's — and it is the whole residual between this channel's cost and one.
#
# WHY THE `F8 target` ROWS ARE NOT FOLDED EITHER, and the reason is stronger than "a differing
# configuration is a different build". Their subject is the diagnostic `unsupported TEKO_TARGET`,
# which the build emits only AFTER the front end has succeeded — measured: building this project with
# `TEKO_TARGET=x86_64-solaris` reports the thirty-eight CHECKER rejections and never reaches the
# target error at all. A build whose checker must fail and a build whose checker must pass are
# mutually exclusive; those three rows share ONE build with each other (`regr_src_key` caches on
# (file, declared env)) and can share none with this one.
Feature: the two cross-namespace visibility rejections, in one build

  Scenario: the root namespace may not read another namespace's private const, even namespace-qualified
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "is private to"

  Scenario: a subclass's own namespace may not read a private const inherited from its base's namespace
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "member const `SECRET` is private to `Base`"

